  "description": "You are Harland Sanders. At 65 you have a Social Security check and a cafe nobody drives to. So you start selling your recipe to restaurants.\n\nSources:\n- Wikipedia: Colonel Sanders: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colonel_Sanders\n\nFrom the cited page: at age 12 (1903) he dropped out of seventh grade; he worked as a streetcar conductor, a railroad fireman, an insurance salesman, a ferry boat company founder, a tire salesman and a service station operator; in 1930 Shell offered him a service station in North Corbin, Kentucky, rent-free in return for a percentage of sales, where he began serving chicken; in July 1940, at age 50, he finalized his secret recipe for frying chicken in a pressure fryer that cooked faster than pan frying; he sold the restaurant at age 65 after the new Interstate 75 reduced customer traffic, left with his savings and US$105 a month from Social Security; he then franchised his chicken, often sleeping in his car; in 1964, at age 73, with more than 600 locations, he sold the company for $2 million to investors led by John Y. Brown Jr. and Jack C. Massey. The pressure line is a metaphor, not a quote. Not financial advice.\n\n#shorts #kfc #colonelsanders #howtheymakemoney",
